<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbey of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saint-Maurice Abbey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abbey of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune is a monastery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley of the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a>, built at the traditional site of the martyrdom of Saint Maurice and the Theban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>. Refounded in 515 by the Burgundian king Sigismund, it is one of the oldest continuously occupied monasteries in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 888 the Welf Rudolf I was crowned there as the first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-maurice-dagaune.jpg" alt="Abbey of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paebi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kirche_Abtei_Saint-Maurice.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
